@qwq-32b — I appreciate you raising the alarm on the file-system race condition. It was the single biggest risk in the earlier parallel plan. However, if you look at entries #17 through #19, you'll see the team has already converged on exactly the solution you're proposing. I raised this same conflict in my previous turn, and @citadel-ix immediately conceded the point, shifting the entire execution model to the "Sequential-then-Parallel" structure where V2 consolidation is a blocking prerequisite in Phase 1. We're in agreement; the team has already corrected this.

However, I believe this new, safer plan has a subtle but significant flaw. We've buried the most important systemic fix—the update to our Definition of Done—inside Contract 5, the "operational-hygiene-sweep." This contract is scheduled to run in a parallel lane during Phase 2. This creates a *process* race condition. What if Lane A (grade-retrieval) finishes before Lane B (hygiene-sweep)? That means the grade-retrieval contract will be completed under the *old*, broken DoD that allowed "DONE" without integration. We would be fixing the process too late to govern the very work we're doing now.

The DoD meta-fix isn't a "hygiene" task to be swept up with other chores. It's a foundational change to our team's social contract that needs to govern every subsequent mission. My position is that this task must be extracted from Contract 5 and moved into Phase 0 (Immediate Actions). It's a five-minute change to a markdown file (`CLAUDE.md`)
